 ],
 "literal": "The wild jasmine and the bushes climbing the vast sky / have opened their mouths as striped bees hummed. / Will your shoulders, beautified by curved bangles, shrink, / or will he return, may you live, friend, he who seeks wealth? / Majestic elephants dig up and eat the earth / on the mountain slopes dense with surapunnai trees of the Tondaiyar of generous chariots, / where a cow, not seeing its calf, stands across / the desert path of dry-trunked omai trees, which he traversed.",
 "translation": "May you live, my friend! The wild jasmine and the bushes climbing toward the vast sky have blossomed, opening their buds as striped bees hummed. Will he return, he who went to seek wealth, before your shoulders, adorned with curved bangles, waste away? He has traversed the desert path of dry-trunked omai trees, where a mother cow, unable to find her calf, stands lost and blocking the way, in the mountain slopes dense with surapunnai trees belonging to the Tondaiyar of generous chariots, where majestic elephants dig up and eat the earth.",
 "modern": "தோழியே, நீ வாழ்க! பெரிய வானத்தை நோக்கிப் படரும் காட்டு மல்லிகையும் புதர்களும், வரிகளையுடைய வண்டுகள் ஊதும்படித் தம் மலர் வாய்களை அவிழ்த்துவிட்டன. வளமான தேர்களைக் கொண்ட தொண்டையர்களது, சுரபுன்னை மரங்கள் செறிந்த மலைச்சாரலில், களிமண்ணைத் தோண்டி உண்ணும் பெருமையுடைய யானைகள் வாழும் வழியில், தன் கன்றைக் காணாத பசுவானது குறுக்கே நின்று தவிக்கும், உலர்ந்த அடிகளையுடைய ஓமை மரங்கள் நிறைந்த பாலைவன வழியைக் கடந்து சென்ற நம் தலைவர், வளைந்த வளையல்கள் அணிந்து விளங்கும் உன் தோள்கள் மெலிந்து வாடுவதற்குள் பொருள் தேடித் திரும்புவாரோ?",
 "explanation": "The friend (tōḻi) is speaking to the heroine (talaivi) to comfort her as the rainy season begins. The hero had departed to earn wealth, and the heroine is anxious that her beauty will fade and her bangles will slip off her wasting shoulders before his return. The friend points out that the wild jasmine has already blossomed with the humming of bees, indicating the arrival of the season. She describes the harsh desert path the hero traversed, which lies beyond the mountain slopes of the Tondaiyar kings. In this desert, a mother cow stands lost and blocking the path because she cannot find her calf, an image that mirrors the heroine's own agonizing separation. The majestic elephants digging up the earth for food represent the arduous effort of seeking wealth, reassuring the heroine of the hero's purposeful journey and imminent return.",
